      "text": "To find the ratio of the shorter height to the original height of the cone, we first need to determine the slant height of the original cone and then use the volume formula for the cone to find the height of the shorter cone.\n\nThe circumference of the base of the original cone is given as \\(16\\pi\\) inches. The circumference \\(C\\) of a circle is given by the formula:\n\\[ C = 2\\pi r \\]\nwhere \\(r\\) is the radius of the base. Therefore, we can find the radius \\(r\\) of the base of the original cone by solving:\n\\[ 16\\pi = 2\\pi r \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{16\\pi}{2\\pi} = 8 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe slant height \\(l\\) of the original cone can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the radius, the height, and the slant height:\n\\[ l^2 = r^2 + h^2 \\]\nwhere \\(h\\) is the height of the cone. For the original cone, \\(h = 30\\) inches:\n\\[ l^2 = 8^2 + 30^2 = 64 + 900 = 964 \\]\n\\[ l = \\sqrt{964} = 2\\sqrt{241} \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe radius of the base of the shorter cone is the same as the radius of the base of the original cone, which is 8 inches. Let the height of the shorter cone be \\(h'\\). The volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h \\]\nFor the original cone, the volume is:\n\\[ V_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8^2) (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) (30) = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\nFor the shorter cone, the volume is given as \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches:\n\\[ V_{\\text{shorter}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8^2) h' = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) h' = \\frac{64}{3} \\pi h' \\]\nWe know that this volume is equal to \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches:\n\\[ \\frac{64}{3} \\pi h' = 192\\pi \\]\nDividing both sides by \\(\\pi\\):\n\\[ \\frac{64}{3} h' = 192 \\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(\\frac{3}{64}\\):\n\\[ h' = 192 \\cdot \\frac{3}{64} = 9 \\text{ inches} \\]\nThe original height \\(h\\) of the cone is 30 inches. The ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[ \\frac{h'}{h} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10} \\]\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}} \\]",
